==English==
{{was wotd|2006|October|17}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|cavalcade}}, from {{der|en|fro|cavalcade}}, from {{der|en|roa-oit|cavalcata}}, from {{m|it|cavalcare|t=to ride on horseback}}, from {{der|en|ML.|caballicō}}, from {{der|en|VL.|caballus|t=horse}}. {{doublet|en|chevauchee}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkævəlˌkeɪd/|/ˌkævəlˈkeɪd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-cavalcade.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-cavalcade.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪd}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|collective}} A [[company]] of [[rider]]s.
#: {{syn|en|company}}
# A [[parade]].
#: {{syn|en|parade|procession}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1887|author=H. Rider Haggard|authorlink=H. Rider Haggard|title=She: A History of Adventure|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/3155/3155-0.txt|passage=For the first three hours all went as well as could be expected, and then an accident happened that nearly lost us the pleasure of the company of our venerable friend Billali, whose litter was leading the '''cavalcade'''.}}
#* '''1929''', [[w:M. Barnard Eldershaw|M. Barnard Eldershaw]], ''[[w:A House is Built|A House Is Built]]'', Chapter IX, Section iii
#*: In the second row of the '''cavalcade''' were Francie, Fanny's god-daughter, now thirteen years old and already elegant in long frilled pantalettes, tartan skirts, and a leghorn hat with streamers, …
# A [[trail ride]], usually more than one day long.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author=[[w:Robert Barr (writer)|Robert Barr]]
|title=Lord Stranleigh Abroad
|chapter=5|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1796924W
|passage=Stranleigh found no difficulty in getting a '''cavalcade''' together at Bleacher’s station, an amazingly long distance west of New York.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[series]], a [[chain]] (e.g. of events).
#: {{syn|en|chain|series}}
#: {{ux|en|As soon as I visited this website, a '''cavalcade''' of dialog boxes started to appear on my screen; that's when I realized my computer was infected with a virus.}}

